# Four experts and Dave

Four experts, each built from a researched list of a real person's own mindsets, decision rules and algorithms
(18–20 numbered items with their words and a source, run `20261013-four-seats` in `~/dev/ancient-games`), and
**Dave, the control**: ten numbered rules written from EJ's description — he loves experiments, is data-driven, and
borrows solutions from nature. Each answers on a different engine, so their agreement is closer to five views than
one model's echo.

| Expert | Engine | Model |
|---|---|---|
| Linus Torvalds | Codex (read-only) | gpt-6.1-sol |
| 范蠡 Fan Li | agy → **Claude** when the task needs web or shell, or when agy fails | gemini-3.8-flash-medium → claude-opus-5-5 |
| 孫子 Sun Tzu | dsh headless | deepseek-flash |
| 老子 Laozi | opencode (plan mode) | MiniMax-M3.1-Flash-Preview |
| **Dave — the control** | Claude | claude-opus-5-5 |

Mapping, fallback and Dave: EJ, 2026-10-13. EJ chose Dave as the control instead of a plain no-persona answer, so
there is no persona-free baseline: a comparison shows what each rule set adds relative to the others, not relative
to a plain model. Why the briefs are built this way (rules applied as instructions, item numbers cited, no invented
facts): `runs/20261013-seats-roleplay/synthesis.md` in the same repo.

## 1. Run

From the project root (opencode can read only files inside it):

```bash
python3 ~/.claude/skills/four-experts/scripts/four_experts.py run "Should we keep Cloudflare analytics on the page?"
```

- `--question-file F` for a long question. Put the facts the experts need **in the question**; they are told not to
  invent missing ones and will list them as questions instead.
- `--needs-web` / `--needs-shell` when the task needs either — **you decide this from the task** — then 范蠡 runs
  on Claude, because agy headless cannot use web or shell (soft-denied, exits 0 with nothing).
- **Inside Claude Code, add `--claude-by-caller`.** The script then leaves Dave (and any 范蠡 fallback) to you: for
  each `CALLER` line it prints, start a subagent with "Read <brief> and do what it says." and save its final message
  to the answer path it names. Outside Claude Code the script runs `claude -p` itself.
- `--only dave` (or any comma list of ids: `linus,fan-li,sun-tzu,lao-tzu,dave`) with `--out <existing run>` adds
  those answers to a run and keeps the others.
- Output: `runs/<date>-four-experts-<slug>/` (or `four-experts/…` when there is no `runs/`): `question.md`,
  `seats/` (the lists used, frozen), `briefs/`, `answers/<id>.md`, `logs/`, `status.json`.
- 范蠡 falls back from agy to Claude automatically when agy returns nothing, an error, a timeout, or an answer
  without the required sections (a plan instead of the answer). `status.json` records the reason; the failed agy
  output is kept as `answers/fan-li.agy-failed.md`.

## 2. Check

```bash
python3 ~/.claude/skills/four-experts/scripts/four_experts.py check <run dir>
python3 ~/.claude/skills/four-experts/scripts/four_experts.py check <run dir> --sabotage   # must report ≥ 2 failures
```

It checks every answer has the five sections, cites at least one item, cites only item numbers its list has, and
writes any run of 4+ Chinese characters exactly as its list or identity file does (on a miss it shows the nearest
passage, so a 趙/赵 script change reads differently from an invention). **What it does not establish:** that a rule
was applied *correctly*, or that the advice is good — read for that. The script was written by Claude; say so when
resting a conclusion on it.

## 3. Compare (the caller writes `compare.md` in the run folder)

```
# Four experts and Dave — <question>

| | Linus | 范蠡 | 孫子 | 老子 | Dave (control) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Answer (one line) | | | | | |
| Main items used | #… | #… | #… | #… | #… |

## Where they agree
## Where they disagree   (side by side, quoted; never averaged)
## What each added that the others did not
## What they need to know   (the questions, merged, for EJ)
## Check   (`check` result; engines and any fallback from status.json)
```

Label each line *quoted* (from an answer), *my reading*, or *not measured*. **EJ decides; the experts advise.**
Do not pick a winner by vote count — five engines answering one brief still share the brief.

## Used by workflow-design (method 3.3 and 3.5)

The workflow-design method calls this skill at two steps (EJ, 2026-10-13), each with only the experts who named it
as where their rules are reliable (`runs/20261013-four-experts-where-in-ej-s-workflow/compare.md`):

| Method step | `--only` | Question template (fill the Facts, pass with `--question-file`) |
|---|---|---|
| 3.3 unclear spots, once the list is written | `linus,sun-tzu,lao-tzu,dave` | `assets/questions/unclear-spots.md` |
| 3.5 before a piece runs as a loop | `linus,fan-li,sun-tzu,dave` | `assets/questions/loop-check.md` |

Their answers are advice: a raised spot is added and marked *raised by*, a "drop it" goes to EJ as a question, a
failure a check would miss strengthens the check or sends the piece back to the unclear list. The design's state file
records the run folder, or *not asked* with the reason.

## Notes

- Plain English Markdown briefs for every engine; replies in English; EJ referred to as they/them (EJ, 2026-10-13).
  Chinese appears only where an expert quotes its own list.
- The skill lives in the Ancient Games repo (`.claude/skills/four-experts/`), linked into `~/.claude/skills` and
  `~/.agents/skills`. The four lists are read from `$FOUR_EXPERTS_SEATS` (default `runs/20261013-four-seats` in
  that repo),
  files `final-opus/<id>.md` and `identity/<id>.md` — the one copy. Dave's are in this skill's `assets/seats/`. Each
  run folder keeps a frozen copy of what was used.
- Engine calls: prompts in files, stdin from `/dev/null`, a timeout per call (`--timeout`, default 1800 s), the
  model named explicitly. Claude calls drop `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` so they use the subscription login.
---
name: requirement-check
description: Evaluate a rule, requirement, threshold or algorithm against requirement-quality criteria before applying or writing it down, and ask the person which criteria it fails. Use when someone states a rule, requirement, constraint, threshold, algorithm or design guideline; when writing or editing a normative line in a document, plan or config; or when a rule reads as arbitrary, unattributed or impossible to check.
---

# Requirement check

A general standard (RFC 2119 / 8174), installed globally — not part of the Ancient Games framework. The
worked failure in `meaningful-names` cites the Ancient Games as one example.

A short gate between **hearing a rule** and **acting on it**. It exists because rules get written down that nobody
can trace, that count two different things at once, or that nothing can ever fail — and the cost lands months later,
when nobody can say whether the rule still means anything.

Run it when someone states a rule, and run it on yourself before writing a normative line into any document.

## The eight questions

Ask them of **one statement**. Say which it passes and which it fails, then **ask the person about the failures** —
do not fill the gaps yourself.

1. **Source.** Who said this, and where? Can you point to the exact line? *"Mine"* is a valid answer. Unlabelled is
   not.
2. **One thing.** It is about **one action or one event**: one thing that happens, and what must be true when it
   does. A condition or a record that belongs to that event is part of it, not a second statement. If it names two
   events (a role is defined, a process starts), it is **two** — split them, and give each its own source. An "and",
   an "or", a "/" or a second number is only a hint to look; it is not the verdict. *(EJ, 2026-10-12.)*
3. **Unit.** Every number says what it counts. Attempts or hours? Roles, or processes running at once? Bytes or
   files?
4. **Can it fail.** Name one concrete input that would make it fail. `TBD`, `—`, `0` and empty must be among the
   failing inputs. If you cannot name one, it is not checkable — it is a wish.
5. **Weak words.** *like, about, appropriate, as needed, logical, fast, enough, reasonable, only has to, TBD.* Replace
   each with a value, **or mark the value open**. Never invent the number.
6. **Level.** Is it **MUST** (a gate that stops work), **SHOULD** (a default that may be overridden with a stated
   reason), or **guidance**? A safety bound earns MUST. **A method does not** — see the RFC note below.
7. **Basis.** Measured, quoted from a source, or a guess? A guess is fine **if it is labelled** — `n=0`, with when it
   will be looked at again.
8. **Links.** Does it claim a relation to another rule — *"half of"*, *"pairs with"*, *"the same as"*, *"the runtime
   part"*? The person probably did not say that. **A link is its own claim and needs its own source.**

## Three rules for the agent

- **Check the line you write, not only the reply you heard.** When the person answers your question briefly ("yes",
  "flag them", "use git"), the requirement is **your question plus their answer**. Write that whole rule — what, when,
  who, what happens next — run the eight questions on the written line, and only then put it in the document. A
  pasted short reply fails *one thing*, *weak words* and *can it fail* on its own. *(EJ, 2026-10-12, after "flag
  them" was written into a design alone and later misread.)*
- **Never fill a gap with your own value.** A missing unit, threshold or test goes back as a question. Inventing it
  is exactly how a rule becomes unattributable later, and the invention is indistinguishable from the original by
  the time anyone notices.
- **Never add structure that was not given.** No halves, no numbered reasons, no ordered pairs, no named phases,
  unless the person said so. Quote them, state the operative sentence, stop. If a relationship between two rules is
  wanted, that is a separate claim with its own evidence.

## Two verified facts, worth quoting

- **RFC 2119 §6, "Guidance in the use of these Imperatives":** imperatives *"must be used with care and sparingly…
  they MUST only be used where it is actually required… they must not be used to try to impose a particular method."*
  Most design rules are method. Method does not earn MUST.
- **RFC 8174 (updates 2119):** the key words *"have the meanings specified herein only when they are in all
  capitals."* Lowercase *must* and *should* carry no defined requirement meaning. If nothing marks which lines are
  requirements, a reader cannot tell rules from explanation.

## How to answer

Evaluate first, in a table or a short list — **pass / FAIL per question**. Then ask the failing ones as questions,
one line each, with the options if you can see them. Two or three real questions beat eight formalities.

Do not report a rule as complete when you chose its missing value. Say which values you left open.

## A worked failure

> *"At most 5 subagent roles per run and at most 3 running at once."*

Four of the eight fail: **Source** — nobody could name one; **One thing** — a count of roles *and* a count of
concurrent processes; **Unit** — two different units, one word ("subagent") used for both; **Level** — a method
preference written as a cap. The sentence would not parse, and it took four days to find out why. The checklist
catches it in thirty seconds.

The same checklist, run on *"No single call runs longer than the ceiling: 8 hours"*, passes all eight — because it
names who set it, counts one thing, uses a unit, and some input (a 40-hour plan) makes it fail.

## Limits

The quality characteristics behind these questions (necessary, appropriate, unambiguous, complete, singular,
feasible, verifiable, correct, conforming) come from requirements-engineering sources — IEEE 830, ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148
and the INCOSE *Guide to Writing Requirements* — which were **not read** when this skill was written: they are
paywalled or blocked, and the list is recalled rather than verified. The two RFC points above **were** read, and the
quotes are checked. Treat the rest as a good working list, not as a citation.

Origin: `docs/research/20261005-requirement-statements/FINDINGS.md` in the Ancient Games project, 2026-10-05.

